 
 
IN THE SECOND JUDICIAL CIRCUIT OF FLORIDA 
OFFICE OF THE CHIEF JUDGE 
ADMINISTRATIVE ORDER 2026-04  
 
IN RE: COURTHOUSE SECURITY 
WHEREAS, citizens should have a reasonable expectation of safety and 
security when they choose to seek resolution of their disputes or to conduct 
business in the Court Facilities in the Second Judicial Circuit; and, 
WHEREAS, the health, safety, and welfare of all citizens in Court Facilities 
and on the grounds of such facilities are a primary concern of the Second Judicial 
Circuit; and, 
WHEREAS, all cases in circuit and county court have the potential for 
violence or to threaten the participants, witnesses, observers, staff, and judges; 
and, 
WHEREAS, pursuant to Rule 2.215(b)(7), Florida Rules of General Practice 
and Judicial Administration, the Chief Judge must regulate the use of all Court 
Facilities; and, 
WHEREAS, pursuant to section 29.008(1), Florida Statutes, and section 
14, Article V of the State Constitution, counties are required to fund the cost of 
security of facilities for the circuit and county courts, to include security for all 
judicial officers, staff, jurors, volunteers of a tenant agency, and the public for 
the circuit and county courts; and,  
WHEREAS, section 29.008(1)(e), Florida Statutes, defines security as 
including, but not limited to, all reasonable and necessary costs of services of 
law enforcement officers or licensed security guards and all electronic, cellular, 
or digital monitoring and screening devices necessary to ensure the safety and 
security of all persons visiting or working in a facility; to provide for security of 
the facility, including protection of property owned by the county or state; and 
for security of prisoners brought to any facility. This includes bailiffs while 
providing courtroom and other security for each judge and other quasi-judicial 
officers; and, 
WHEREAS, pursuant to section 30.15(1)(c), Florida Statues, Sheriffs, in 
their respective counties, in person or by deputy, shall attend all sessions of the 
circuit court and county court held in their counties; and, 
WHEREAS, it is necessary and appropriate to regulate the possession 
of firearms, weapons, and other destructive devices within and on the grounds 
of any Court Facility in the Second Judicial Circuit to protect members of the 
public and court employees, to ensure a safe forum for the redress of legal issues, 
and to provide for the orderly and safe administration of justice; and, 
WHEREAS, section 790.06 (12), Florida Statutes, prohibits any person to 
openly carry a handgun or carry a concealed weapon or concealed firearm into 
any Courthouse or courtroom, regardless of a license to carry concealed weapons 
under that chapter; and, 
 
 
 
 

