WHEREAS, pursuant to the authority provided to the Chief Judge by the 
Florida Constitution, section 2(d), Article V; section 43.26, Florida Statutes; and 
Rule 2.215(b), Florida Rules of General Practice and Judicial Administration the 
Chief Judge is responsible for the administrative supervision of the courts, and 
to promote the prompt and efficient administration of justice in all courts within 
the circuit; it is therefore:  
ORDERED, effective immediately: 
1. This administrative order directs reasonable and necessary Courthouse1 
security in the Second Judicial Circuit in order to ensure the safety and 
security of: (a) all members of the public seeking a resolution to their 
disputes through the courts, (b) all persons seeking to conduct business in 
a Court Facility, (c) the judiciary and their staff, (d) court employees, and 
(e) property owned by the county or state.  
 
2. All visitors entering any Courthouse, courtroom, hearing room, or 
chambers will be screened by a bailiff prior to entering. The bailiff’s unit is 
responsible for conducting screenings. Screenings shall be sufficient to 
ensure that no firearms, weapons, destructive devices, hazardous 
materials, chemical agents, or other items deemed dangerous by the 
bailiff’s unit enter the Courthouse. Minimum screening requirements shall 
include a bailiff to conduct the screening, use of a metal detector, a barrier 
to limit free ingress, and a search of personal articles of sufficient size to 
hide a weapon. An x-ray machine may be used in lieu of a personal search. 
 
3. All circuit and county judges, general magistrates, and hearing officers 
shall have a bailiff in attendance, at all times while conducting hearings in 
courtrooms, and hearing rooms. This bailiff shall remain in attendance 
until the docket is completed and they are dismissed by the presiding judge, 
general magistrate, or hearing officer. For proceedings in chambers, it is 
within the discretion of the presiding judge, general magistrate, or hearing 
officer to require the presence of a bailiff. 
 
4. All circuit and county judges, general magistrates, hearing officers, their 
judicial assistants, and chamber receptionists shall have personal and 
continuously monitored panic alarms to call for immediate law enforcement 
assistance if necessary. The panic alarms will be wireless and transmit an 
alarm to the county 911 office or the Courthouse bailiff station. The panic 
alarm system will be tested, with prior coordination between the judicial 
officer and the bailiff's unit, once per quarter. Results of the test will be 
given to the Chief Judge or his designee by the bailiff’ unit.  
 
5. Each courtroom, hearing room, judicial chambers, and general magistrate 
and hearing officer offices shall have a functional telephone and a panic 
alarm.  
 
 
1 “Courthouse” or “Court Facility” is defined as any County Courthouse, Courthouse Annex, or 
other building designated by the Chief Judge, where court is held in the Second Judicial Circuit. 
Facility is further defined in section 29.008(1)(a).  

