6. All persons are prohibited from bringing any firearms, weapons, 
destructive devices, hazardous materials, chemical agents, or other items 
deemed dangerous by the bailiff’s unit into any Courthouse except as 
provided herein. Any person who carries these items into the Courthouse 
and refuses to surrender the item shall be denied entry and may be subject 
to arrest in accordance with applicable provisions of Florida Statutes. 
Firearms, weapons, and destructive devices are defined in accordance with 
the Florida Statutes.  
 
7. Federal, state, and local law enforcement officers in uniform are authorized 
to carry weapons or firearms into and within a Courthouse. Nothing herein 
shall be interpreted as countermanding any policy of the agency employing 
the officer.  Any law enforcement officer entering the Courthouse in plain 
clothes may be subject to screening and other bailiff’s unit security 
protocols.  
 
8. However, no federal, state, or local law enforcement officer who is a party to 
a case shall bring weapons or firearms into, or possess within, a Courthouse. 
All officers who are a party to a case shall leave all weapons and firearms 
outside the Courthouse. However, the bailiff’s unit may, in an exercise of 
discretion, allow such officers to relinquish weapons and firearms at the 
bailiff station for temporary safekeeping.  
 
9. Notwithstanding any other provision in this Administrative Order, presiding 
judges have the absolute discretion, pursuant to section 790.06(12), Florida 
Statutes, to prohibit any person, including federal, state, and local law 
enforcement officers, from bringing weapons or firearms into the presiding 
judge’s courtroom. If the presiding judge requires a law enforcement officer 
to relinquish the officer’s weapons or firearms prior to entering the judge’s 
courtroom, the officer shall surrender any such weapons or firearms to the 
bailiff for temporary safekeeping. 
 
10. Armored car service personnel on official business for the Office of the Clerk 
of the Circuit Court are authorized to carry weapons or firearms into and 
within the Courthouse and in secure areas within the Courthouse.  
 
11. All circuit and county judges, as authorized by law, have the authority to 
carry concealed weapons or firearms into any Courthouse in the Second 
Judicial Circuit. The Chief Judge may authorize in writing any officer of the 
court or other similar person to carry a weapon or firearm into a Courthouse 
in the Second Judicial Circuit. 
 
12. All circuit judges, Leon County judges, general magistrates, circuit hearing 
officers, judicial assistants, and designated court administration staff shall 
have secure parking spaces in the underground parking garage of the Leon 
County Courthouse.  
 
 

